Subject: [PATCH 0003] chat: remove upstream's bundled agent wiring
Refs: ADR-0010, ADR-0009
Status: UNWRITTEN — header and intent only, no diff hunks. See below.

STATUS
======

This file contains no diff. It is disabled by its `.patch.no` suffix.

Of the six patches in this series, this is the one that most needs to be written
against source someone has actually read, and the one where invented hunks would
do the most damage. See RISK below: the target directory took 67 commits in 5.8
months.

INTENT
======

Stop upstream's bundled chat agent from registering itself, so that it does not
compete with Adze for the same panels, commands, and keybindings — and so that a
build produced by this pipeline has no chat provider reaching a network endpoint
we did not configure.

The configuration half is already done: apps/ide/branding/product.delta.jq deletes
defaultChatAgent, chatParticipantRegistry, gitHubEntitlement, chatEntitlementUrl,
and aiEvaluationUrl. Without those keys, upstream's chat surfaces largely
self-disable, because the entitlement flow has nothing to point at.

This patch handles the remainder: view container registrations and command
contributions that exist independently of product.json, and which would otherwise
leave a second empty chat panel in the sidebar next to ours.

FILES
=====

  src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/chat.contribution.ts
  src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/chatSetup.ts
  src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/actions/**
  possibly src/vs/workbench/contrib/inlineChat/browser/inlineChat.contribution.ts

Marked "possibly" because whether inline chat needs separate treatment depends on
whether upstream still gates it on the same entitlement, and that has changed more
than once.

RISK: THIS IS THE HOT ONE
=========================

From the churn table in ADR-0010, measured over 5.8 months and ~24 releases:

  src/vs/workbench/contrib/inlineChat/    67 commits

That is the highest churn measured anywhere in the blast radius. A line-pinned
patch here should be expected to break at a meaningful fraction of upstream's ~50
releases per year, and each break costs a human a .rej resolution inside code they
did not write and are not tracking.

Three consequences follow, and they are the design:

  1. **Delete registrations, never edit logic.** A patch that removes a
     `registerAction2(...)` call or a `Registry.as(...).registerWorkbenchContribution(...)`
     line survives upstream refactoring the implementation behind it. A patch that
     modifies the implementation does not.

  2. **Prefer configuration to code.** Every key the delta can remove is a hunk
     this patch does not need. If a future upstream release adds a product.json
     switch that disables the bundled agent, this patch should shrink to nothing.

  3. **This is the argument for AHP.** ADR-0010 chose to run Adze's agent behind
     upstream's Agent Host Protocol specifically so that we are not maintaining a
     bespoke chat UI against this directory. The honest accounting: AHP does not
     make this patch unnecessary — we still have to turn upstream's agent off — but
     it caps the exposure at "one deletion patch" instead of "a parallel
     implementation of a subsystem upstream ships weekly".

If this patch becomes expensive to maintain, the correct response is to shrink it,
not to grow it. Leaving upstream's chat panel visible and empty is a worse product
but a cheaper fork, and that trade is available at any time.

HOW TO WRITE IT
===============

  1. Fetch the checkout and generate product.json with the delta applied.
  2. Build once with no patch here at all, and look at what upstream's chat
     surfaces do when defaultChatAgent is absent. That measurement decides how
     much this patch needs to remove, and it may be very little.
  3. Remove only registration calls. Record in the patch header which upstream
     symbols were referenced, so the next break is diagnosable without a bisect.
  4. git -C apps/ide/vscode diff adze/pristine -- src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat \
       > apps/ide/patches/0003-remove-bundled-chat-agent-wiring.patch

Step 2 is not optional. It is the difference between a patch sized to the problem
and a patch sized to an assumption.
